--- /dev/null
+++ b/.crit-loop/round-3/crit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+**First read.** The eye lands on the headline "Tide times your harbor office can stand behind". It then goes right to the grey "Today at Porthmellin" panel and comes back to the dark "Set up your harbor" button. The three feature notes below read as a quiet footnote row. The page looks like a calm, plain tool for a harbor office: easy to trust, but it does not show much. On the phone and tablet the panel drops below the button and the three notes stack. On the tablet the panel now spans the full width to the nav's right edge.
+
+**What it's for.** It gets a harbor office to set up Tidewell. To do that it must show that one page can carry tides, lock gate times, pontoon depth and notices for berth holders.
+
+**Verdict.** The fixes closed the layout findings: the panel lines up with the third feature column, the tablet panel reaches the nav's right edge, and the tablet hero paragraph is now 67 characters per line. The biggest problem has not changed. The sample still shows only the four tide rows that any tide almanac gives, so it does not show what makes Tidewell different.
+
+**Finding status**
+- sample-shows-tides-only: open. At all four viewports the panel still has four tide rows only. It has no gate-time row, no pontoon depth, no notice, and no before-and-after against how the office posts these now.
+- phone-notes-short-lines: open. On the phone the three feature notes still measure 43, 41 and 47 characters per line, so two are still under 45. They read normally at this width.
+- tablet-right-band: closed. On the tablet the panel now runs from x 64 to x 704, which is the nav's right edge. The hero text and the feature notes stop at x 576 to keep a readable line length. With the full-width panel below them, that reads as ordinary ragged right space, not an empty band.
+- tablet-hero-measure: closed. The tablet hero paragraph now measures 67 characters per line, down from 77.
+- panel-off-feature-grid: closed. The panel's left edge is x 837 on desktop and x 1157 on widescreen, the same as the third feature column at both widths.
+
+**New problems.** None.
+
+**What would move it most**
+1. The sample panel shows tide times only. This is the "Today at Porthmellin" panel at every viewport. A harbor master sees a table their almanac already gives them. Lock gate times, pontoon depth and notices appear only as text claims in the row below, and nothing compares Tidewell with how the office posts these today. The fix is to add a gate-time row, a pontoon depth reading, one notice, and a short before-and-after against the office's current method. The maker must supply the real gate times, depth figures, notice text and current method. Taste. Viewports: desktop, phone, tablet, widescreen.
+
+**Smaller things.**
+- On desktop and widescreen the panel moved right and got narrower (299 px). This left a gap of about 277 px between the hero text and the panel. The page now looks a little split in two, but it reads clearly. If it matters, widen the hero text column a little toward the panel. Viewports: desktop, widescreen.
+- The second and first feature notes on the phone measure 41 and 43 characters per line, under 45. Reduce the side padding a little, or accept the shortfall at this width. Viewports: phone.
+
+**What's working.**
+- The panel now shares the feature row's grid on desktop and widescreen. The two rows lock together and the page looks deliberate.
+- There is one accent color, used only on the button. Every text block has contrast of 6.67:1 or more. The eye has one clear place to act.
+- The copy is specific to the trade: "lock gate times", "visitor pontoon", "before the first boat asks". It names real harbor work and does not use generic SaaS phrases.
