--- /dev/null
+++ b/.crit-loop/round-4/crit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+**First read.** On desktop and widescreen the eye goes first to the headline, which is now larger and runs across most of the left column. Next it goes to the grey "Today at Porthmellin" panel on the right, then to the dark "Set up your harbor" button, and last to the three feature notes. The page reads as a calm, sober tool for harbor offices: it is plain, it has a quiet palette, and you can trust what it says. The hero now feels like one block and less like two halves. On tablet and phone the panel stacks below the button and the order stays the same, so the eye goes headline, button, tide table, notes.
+
+**What it's for.** It must make a harbor master want to click "Set up your harbor" because Tidewell gives them more than their almanac does (this comes from the work order's crit).
+
+**Verdict.** The layout fix helped: the hero no longer looks split in two. But the most important problem has not changed. The sample panel still shows only four tide rows, so the page still does not show the thing that makes Tidewell worth having.
+
+**Finding status**
+- sample-shows-tides-only: open. At all four viewports the panel still holds only Low water and High water rows with time and height. It has no gate-time row, no pontoon depth, no notice, and no before-and-after comparison.
+- hero-panel-gap: closed (by eye). The hero paragraph is now 480 px wide (it was 416 px), and the headline now reaches about x=716 on desktop. The gap between the paragraph and the panel is now about 213 px (it was about 277 px). On desktop and widescreen the hero now reads as one composition.
+- phone-notes-short-lines: open. On the phone the facts file still measures 43 and 41 characters per line for the first and second feature notes, which is below the target of 45. They still read normally at this width.
+
+**New problems.** The wider hero paragraph now ends with "quay." alone on its third line on desktop, tablet and widescreen. This is a small widow, not a problem that changes the piece. The fix added no new elements, so there is no new template tell.
+
+**What would move it most**
+1. The sample panel shows only tide times (sample-shows-tides-only). This is the "Today at Porthmellin" panel at every viewport. A harbor master sees a table that their almanac already gives them. The things that make Tidewell different (lock gate times, depth at the pontoon, notices) appear only as text claims in the row of notes below, and nothing compares Tidewell with how the office posts this information today. The fix is to add a gate-time row, a pontoon depth reading, one notice, and a short before-and-after against the office's current method. The maker must supply the real gate times, depth figures, notice text and current method. Do not invent them. Taste. Viewports: desktop, phone, tablet, widescreen.
+
+**Smaller things.**
+- Hero paragraph: "quay." sits alone on the last line. Make the paragraph a little narrower or a little wider so the last line has at least two words. Viewports: desktop, tablet, widescreen.
+- Feature notes on the phone: the first two run at 43 and 41 characters per line. Reduce the side padding a little, or accept the shortfall at this width. Viewports: phone.
+
+**What's working.**
+- The tide table is easy to read. The times and heights are right-aligned, the rules are thin, and the headers are quiet, so the numbers carry the panel at every viewport.
+- There is one accent color, used only on the "Set up your harbor" button. This keeps one clear action on the page.
+- The larger headline now gives the left column enough weight to balance the panel, and this is what closed the gap.
