+++ b/.crit-loop/round-5/crit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+**First read.** On every viewport the eye goes first to the dark headline, "Tide times your harbor office can stand behind". Next it goes to the teal "Set up your harbor" button. On the wider screens it then crosses to the grey "Today at Porthmellin" panel. The page reads as a quiet, plain product page for harbor offices, and it is calm and trustworthy. On the phone and the tablet the panel drops below the button, and the three feature notes stack into one column. The order and the feel stay the same.
+
+**What it's for.** To get a harbor master or harbor office to set up Tidewell, so berth holders can check tides, lock gate times, depth and notices on one page. This comes from the work order's crit.
+
+**Verdict.** This round fixed the line breaks: the hero paragraph no longer ends on a single word. It did not change what the page shows. The biggest thing still in the way is the sample panel. It shows only low and high water, which any almanac gives, so the things that make Tidewell different are still only claims in text.
+
+**Finding status**
+- sample-shows-tides-only: open. The "Today at Porthmellin" panel still holds only four low and high water rows with time and height, at all four viewports. It has no gate-time row, no pontoon depth, no notice, and no before-and-after.
+- hero-paragraph-widow: closed. On desktop, tablet and widescreen the hero paragraph now ends with "the quay.", which is two words.
+- phone-notes-short-lines: open. On the phone the first two feature notes still measure 43 and 41 characters per line, against the target of 45 or more. The phone layout did not change.
+
+**New problems.** None. The after image adds no new elements. The narrower paragraph also fixed the tablet's second feature note, which used to end on the single word "day." and now ends on "the day.".
+
+**What would move it most**
+1. The sample panel shows only tide times. This is the "Today at Porthmellin" panel, to the right of the hero on desktop and widescreen and below the button on tablet and phone. A harbor master sees a table that their almanac already gives them. The three things Tidewell adds (lock gate times, pontoon depth and notices) appear only as text in the notes below, and nothing compares Tidewell with how the office posts this today. To fix it, add a gate-time row, a pontoon depth reading and one notice to the panel, plus a short before-and-after against the office's current method. The maker must supply the real gate times, depth figures, notice text and current method. Do not invent them. Taste. Viewports: desktop, phone, tablet, widescreen.
+
+**Smaller things.**
+- The first two feature notes on the phone run at 43 and 41 characters per line. They still read normally. To reach 45, reduce the side padding a little, or accept the shortfall at this width. Viewports: phone.
+
+**What's working.**
+- The tide table is easy to read. Its numbers align on the right in their columns, the dividing lines are hairlines, and the header labels are muted. You can find a time and a height at a glance.
+- The page has one clear action, the teal "Set up your harbor" button. Nothing competes with it.
+- The copy is specific and plain ("posted before the first boat asks", "Enter one sounding"). It talks about harbor work, not general benefits.
